# 3. USER ROLES AND ACCESS CONTROL

## A. ADMIN / MANAGER

The administrator can:

- Access the administrative dashboard.
- Create employee accounts.
- View all employees.
- Edit employee information.
- Activate or deactivate employee accounts.
- Delete employees through a carefully designed process.
- Assign tasks to one or more employees.
- View all tasks and their histories.
- Edit task details.
- Change task priorities and deadlines.
- Monitor employee progress.
- Receive notifications about task activities.
- Review submitted work.
- Approve or reject completion submissions.
- View performance rankings.
- Filter and search employees and tasks.
- View reports and analytics.
- Access the activity audit log.
- Configure basic organization settings.

Administrative privileges must be enforced server-side.

## B. EMPLOYEE

An employee can:

- Sign in using their email and password.
- View their personal dashboard.
- View only tasks they are authorized to access.
- Open a task's details.
- Start an assigned task.
- Update task progress.
- Add progress notes describing completed work.
- View their task history.
- Submit a task for completion review.
- View the outcome of a manager's review.
- Receive relevant notifications.
- Edit permitted profile fields.
- Change their password through a secure authentication flow.

Employees must never be able to:

- Access the administrator dashboard.
- Create or promote themselves to administrator.
- View private information belonging to other employees.
- Change another employee's tasks.
- Assign tasks to themselves or others.
- Modify task approval decisions.
- Modify their own leaderboard score or ranking directly.
- Delete audit records.
- Modify trusted role information through ordinary profile updates.

Implement deny-by-default authorization.

