Create a professional, responsive administrative dashboard.

The dashboard should provide a quick overview of the organization.

## A. Summary Cards

Display:

- Total employees.
- Active employees.
- Total assigned tasks.
- Tasks not yet started.
- Tasks in progress.
- Tasks awaiting review.
- Completed and approved tasks.
- Overdue tasks.
- Overall task completion rate.

Every metric must be calculated from real database records, not hardcoded values.

Define the calculation for each metric clearly.

For example:

Overall completion rate =
Number of approved completed task assignments
divided by
Total task assignments included in the selected reporting period,
multiplied by 100.

Exclude canceled assignments from the denominator and clearly document any other exclusions.

## B. Employee Table

Display:

- Employee name.
- Email.
- Department.
- Job title.
- Account status.
- Total assigned tasks.
- Approved completed tasks.
- In-progress tasks.
- Overdue tasks.
- Completion rate.
- Last activity.
- Actions.

Provide:

- Search by name or email.
- Filtering by department and status.
- Sorting by completion rate, completed tasks, and recent activity.
- Pagination.
- A detailed employee profile page.
- A button to open the employee's assigned tasks.

Use clear visual indicators:

- Green: completed and approved.
- Blue: in progress.
- Gray: not started.
- Amber: awaiting review.
- Red: overdue or requiring attention.

Do not communicate status through color alone. Include text labels and accessible icons.

## C. Task Management

Create a dedicated task management interface.

The administrator can create a task with:

- Task title.
- Detailed description.
- Priority: Low, Medium, High, Critical.
- Start date.
- Deadline.
- Optional estimated effort.
- Assigned employee or employees.
- Optional task category.
- Optional checklist or subtasks.
- Optional completion criteria.
- Optional attachments.

Support assigning one task to multiple employees.

IMPORTANT:
When a task is assigned to multiple employees, each employee must have an independent assignment record and independent progress percentage.

For example:

Task: Build a customer registration page.

Employee A: 100% complete.
Employee B: 60% complete.
Employee C: 20% complete.

The system must not combine their progress into one shared progress value.

The administrator must be able to:

- Edit task details.
- Reassign employees.
- Change deadlines.
- Change priorities.
- Cancel assignments with an audit record.
- View individual progress histories.
- Open each employee's progress updates.
- Review submitted completion evidence.
- Approve or reject submitted work.

Avoid silently overwriting historical information when tasks are edited.

## D. Live Progress Monitoring

Provide a task monitoring interface showing:

- Employee name.
- Task title.
- Current status.
- Progress percentage.
- Latest progress note.
- Last update timestamp.
- Deadline.
- Overdue indicator.
- Completion submission status.

Progress bars must reflect actual persisted values.

When an employee updates progress, the administrator's interface should update automatically using Supabase Realtime or another reliable mechanism.

Implement appropriate subscriptions, cleanup, error handling, and reconnection behavior.

Do not rely on realtime events alone for data integrity. Persist changes first and use the database as the source of truth.

Provide a manual refresh fallback.

